  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole contains the Avogadro number of particles — 6.02 × 10²³ — the number of atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Avogadro's constant is 6.02 × 10²³ particles per mole.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = m ÷ M = 8 ÷ 32 = 0.25 mol; using 16 instead of 32 is the commonest error.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    m = n × M = 0.5 × 100 = 50 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    N = n × 6.02 × 10²³ = 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    At s.t.p. (273 K, 760 mmHg) the molar volume is 22.4 dm³; at r.t.p. it is 24 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = V ÷ 22.4 = 11.2 ÷ 22.4 = 0.5 mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V = 0.25 dm³; n = c × V = 0.2 × 0.25 = 0.05 mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Moles are mass divided by molar mass; rearranged, m = n × M.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The empirical formula is the simplest ratio; the molecular formula is the actual number.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Molecular formula = (empirical formula) × n, where n = M ÷ empirical mass.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    16 ÷ 18 × 100 = 88.9 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2(1) + 32 + 4(16) = 98.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    40 + 2(16 + 1) = 74.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In a closed system no mass is created or destroyed in a chemical reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A given compound has a fixed composition by mass whatever its source.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In CO and CO₂ the masses of oxygen combining with 12 g of carbon are 16 g and 32 g — a ratio of 1 : 2.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = 4 ÷ 40 = 0.1 mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole of any substance has a mass in grams equal to its relative molecular mass.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole of NaCl contains one mole of Na⁺ and one mole of Cl⁻ ions, so 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ ions.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Empirical mass = 30; n = 180 ÷ 30 = 6, so (CH₂O)₆ = C₆H₁₂O₆.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The reaction stops when the limiting reagent runs out.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = 18 ÷ 18 = 1 mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    V = n × 24 = 0.25 × 24 = 6.0 dm³ at r.t.p.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    m = 2 × 18 = 36 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    22.4 dm³ at s.t.p. is one mole, which contains 6.02 × 10²³ molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ionic compounds do not form molecules, so the sum is called the relative formula mass.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each formula unit has four oxygen atoms, so 0.5 × 4 = 2 mol of oxygen atoms.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    14 ÷ 17 × 100 = 82.4 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ratio O₂ : H₂O is 1 : 2, so 1 mol of oxygen gives 2 mol of water.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
